Problem statement:
# String Palindrome Check

Given a string, check whether it is a palindrome. A palindrome reads the same forward and backward.

Print `YES` if the string is a palindrome; otherwise, print `NO`.

### Input Format
A single string containing lowercase English letters.

### Output Format
Print `YES` or `NO`.

### Constraints
- 1 ≤ length of string ≤ 100000
- String contains only lowercase English letters.

### Sample Input
```text
madam
```

### Sample Output
```text
YES
```

Sample 1 input:
```
madam
```
Sample 1 output:
```
YES
```

Sample 2 input:
```
hello
```
Sample 2 output:
```
NO
```

Your previous program is below. On sample 1 it printed
```
NO
```
but the expected output is
```
YES
```
Find the bug and reply with the corrected complete program.
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main() {
    char str[100001];

    // Read the string from standard input
    fgets(str, sizeof(str), stdin);

    // Remove the newline character if present
    size_t len = strlen(str);
    if (len > 0 && str[len - 1] == '\n') {
        str[len - 1] = '\0';
    }

    // Check if the string is a palindrome
    int i, j;
    for (i = 0, j = len - 1; i < j; ++i, --j) {
        if (str[i] != str[j]) {
            printf("NO\n");
            return 0;
        }
    }

    // If all characters match, the string is a palindrome
    printf("YES\n");

    return 0;
}
```

